      {
        "@language": "es",
        "@value": "monzosienita nefelínica"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "nepheline monzosyenite"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#relatedMatch": [
      {
        "@id": "http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/LithologyValue/foidMonzosyenite"
      },
      {
        "@id": "http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/lithology/foid_monzosyenite"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#topConceptOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/material-igme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/adms#status": [
      {
        "@value": "Válido"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/material-igme/caliza",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/created": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date",
        "@value": "2026-03-01"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/material-igme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition": [
      {
        "@language": "es",
        "@value": "Roca sedimentaria carbonatada cuyo constituyente principal es el carbonato cálcico, de los componentes restantes, el más frecuente y dominante es el carbonato de magnesio, siendo accesorios los silicatos o los productos de su alteración como arcillas y sílice, así como la pirita y la siderita. La variedad pura tiene, al menos, un 95% de CaCO3 y las variedades impuras, más del 10% de dolomita o de impurezas. Su origen puede ser predominantemente biológico, químico o mixto"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sedimentary carbonate rock whose principal constituent is calcium carbonate; of the remaining components, magnesium carbonate is the most frequent and dominant, with silicates or their alteration products (clay and silica) as well as pyrite and siderite being accessory. The pure variety has at least 95% CaCO3 and the impure varieties more than 10% dolomite or impurities. Its origin may be predominantly biological, chemical or mixed"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#exactMatch": [
      {
        "@id": "http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/LithologyValue/limestone"
      },
      {
        "@id": "http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/lithology/limestone"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme": [
      {
        "@id": "https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/material-igme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel": [
      {
        "@language": "es",
        "@value": "caliza"
      },
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "limestone"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#topConceptOf": [
      {
        "@id": "https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/material-igme"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/adms#status": [
      {
        "@value": "Válido"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/material-igme/essexita",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"
    ],
    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/created": [
      {
        "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date",
